INT. NATURAL HISTORY MUSEUM - TAXIDERMY HALL - NIGHT

Glass cases. A stuffed polar bear rears in the centre. Glass eyes catch the exit-sign glow.

A NIGHT GUARD, 60s, soft belly, thermos in hand, shuffles down the aisle. He pauses at the bear. Pats its paw.

NIGHT GUARD
Evening, Boris.

A creak behind him. He turns.

A WOMAN in a grey coat stands between two cases. Calm. Hands in pockets.

NIGHT GUARD (CONT'D)
Ma'am. We're closed.

WOMAN
I know.

NIGHT GUARD
Then how did you...

WOMAN
You let me in. At six. When I said I'd left my glasses.

He frowns. Looks at the bear. Looks back.

NIGHT GUARD
Right. Right, I remember. Thought you'd gone.

WOMAN
I thought you'd gone.

She smiles. Steps closer. He sips from the thermos. His shoulders loosen.

NIGHT GUARD
Well. Take your time. Find 'em?

WOMAN
Not yet.

She walks past him, trailing a hand along the glass. Stops at the bear. Reaches into its open jaw. Withdraws a small silver key.

NIGHT GUARD
Hey. What's that?

The Woman turns. Her smile is gone.

WOMAN
Barnaby. Sit down.

He sits, heavily, on the bench.

BARNABY
How do you know my name?

WOMAN
You told me at six.

BARNABY
I didn't.

WOMAN
You wouldn't remember. It was in the tea.

BARNABY
Who are you?

WOMAN
Nell.

Barnaby's thermos slides from his hand. Hits the floor. Nell steps over it. The bear's eyes gleam.
